  bootwright / controller rebind
      Rewrite the host identity the controller record holds to this host's, once, when this
      host keeps the same state store under a changed machine-id, DMI product UUID or root
      filesystem UUID. It refuses a store whose directories are not the ones its registry
      attributed and an identity that did not change, and keeps every binding and reservation.
      A byte-for-byte clone, such as a disk image, snapshot or cloned VM, keeps those
      directories, so it cannot tell a clone from the store: the prompt names the recorded and
      the new identity and asks you to affirm that the recorded host no longer runs this store;
      --yes rebinds only with --recorded-host-retired and this host's three values named, which
      no output repeats.

  Usage
    bootwright controller rebind [flags]

  Command flags
      --machine-id             Name this host's machine-id; required with --yes
      --product-uuid           Name this host's DMI product UUID; required with --yes
      --recorded-host-retired  Affirm the recorded host no longer runs this store; required
                               with --yes
      --root-uuid              Name this host's root filesystem UUID; required with --yes
      --yes                    Confirm the command's ordinary confirmation

  Common flags
      --ascii       Use ASCII status markers
      --color       Select terminal colors (values: auto, always, never) · default auto
      --detail      Select human detail (values: compact, full) · default compact
      -h, --help    Show help for this command
      -o, --output  Select output mode (values: text, json, yaml) · default text
      --progress    Select progress animation (values: auto, always, never) · default auto
      --width       Set presentation width (0: automatic) · default 0

  Examples
    bootwright controller rebind
